    "description": "The SLK Neo 2.0 set is where we would point most new players, because it solves the whole first-week shopping list at once and comes from a brand that also makes serious tournament paddles. A Pickleheads review lists the set at $79.99 for two paddles, a bag and four balls, which puts each paddle well under the price of a single mid-range model. The same review gives a weight of 7.5 ounces, a 13mm polymer honeycomb core, a 15.67-inch by 7.8-inch face, a 5.25-inch handle and a 4.25-inch grip circumference. That is a standard-shape, mid-to-light paddle with a thin core, which tends to feel lively rather than dead, and the 4.25-inch grip is the size most adult beginners fall into. The one caution is that listings disagree on details: Pickleheads describes an SX3 core and a graphite and fiberglass blend face, while at least one retailer lists an SX4 core and a graphite face, so check the exact version on the box. Either way, it is a sensible, forgiving starting point for two people.",
    "pros": [
      "Two paddles, four balls and a bag for $79.99, per a Pickleheads review",
      "Listed at 7.5 oz, light enough to stay quick at the kitchen line",
      "Standard 15.67 x 7.8 in shape with a 4.25 in grip, the most common adult size",
      "Made by Selkirk, which also builds tournament-level paddles"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Core and face material differ between listings (SX3 vs SX4, blend vs graphite)",
      "13mm core gives more pop and less dwell than a 16mm control paddle",
      "Lighter weight offers less plow-through on drives"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The SLK Neo 2.0 set is the easiest way for two beginners to start: Pickleheads lists two 7.5 oz paddles, a bag and four balls for $79.99."
  },
  {
    "id": "onix-graphite-z5",
    "name": "Onix Graphite Z5 Pickleball Paddle",
    "badge": "Best Single Paddle",
    "category": "Widebody, Nomex Honeycomb Core, Graphite Face",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "The Graphite Z5 has been on beginner shortlists for years for one reason: it is wide. Retailer listings give a widebody shape of roughly 15.5 by 8.3 inches, which spreads the hitting area sideways where new players most often mishit, and a mid weight of 7.5 to 8.2 ounces depending on the unit. Onix uses a Nomex honeycomb core and a graphite face, and listings give a 4.25-inch grip circumference and a 5-inch handle. Nomex cores are firmer and louder than polymer cores, so the Z5 feels crisp and has a noticeable pop off the face, which helps beginners get the ball deep without a big swing. The trade-off is that it is less soft at the kitchen line than a thick polymer paddle, and the sound may matter if you play in a neighborhood with noise complaints. Retailers list it as approved for USA Pickleball tournament play. If you only want one good paddle and you value a forgiving face over fine touch, this is the one.",
    "pros": [
      "Widebody 15.5 x 8.3 in face, per retailers, forgives off-center hits",
      "Mid weight listed at 7.5 to 8.2 oz",
      "Graphite face and Nomex core give easy depth on serves and returns",
      "Listed as USA Pickleball approved for tournament play"
