    "pros": [
      "Sold in sets of two or four with balls and a bag",
      "Fiberglass face over a polypropylene honeycomb core",
      "4.25 in grip, the common adult size",
      "Listed as USA Pickleball approved"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Listed weights and set contents vary between sources",
      "Generic build with less consistency than name brands",
      "You will likely outgrow it within a season of regular play"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The Niupipo set is the cheapest sensible way for a family to try pickleball, as long as you confirm the paddle count and ball mix on the listing."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Pickleball Paddles for Beginners",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-pickleball-paddle-for-beginners/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What weight pickleball paddle is best for beginners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most beginners do best with a mid-weight paddle of roughly 7.6 to 8.2 ounces. It gives enough stability to keep off-center hits on target without tiring your arm. Players with shoulder or elbow issues can go slightly lighter, while strong baseline players can go to the top of the range."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a widebody or elongated paddle better for beginners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Widebody or standard shapes are better for most beginners because they offer a wider sweet spot, which forgives the side-to-side mishits new players make most often. Elongated paddles add reach but narrow the sweet spot, so they suit players coming from tennis or squash who already make consistent contact."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much should I spend on my first pickleball paddle?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Somewhere between about $50 and $120 is plenty for a first paddle, and two-player sets in that range cover two people. Premium paddles above $150 add features like raw carbon faces and thermoformed construction that mostly matter once your strokes are consistent."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should beginners buy a pickleball paddle set?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A set is a good buy if two people are starting together, because it usually costs less than two separate paddles and includes balls and a bag. If players want different shapes or weights, buy single paddles instead so each person can choose."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do beginner paddles need to be USA Pickleball approved?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For casual play, no. But approval means the paddle meets the rules for sanctioned play, so you can use it in leagues and local tournaments without swapping it. Every paddle in this guide is listed as approved by its maker or retailers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What core thickness is best for a beginner paddle?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Thicker cores, around 14 to 16mm, tend to feel softer and more controlled, which helps beginners keep the ball low. Thinner 13mm cores feel livelier and add pop. Either is fine for learning; if you want maximum control, lean toward 16mm."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a beginner pickleball paddle last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "With regular play, a beginner paddle typically lasts a season or more before the face texture wears or a dead spot develops. Many players upgrade before the paddle wears out, simply because their game has outgrown it."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Outdoor Gear",
    tags: [
      "best pickleball paddle for beginners",
      "beginner pickleball paddle",
      "pickleball paddle set",
      "best pickleball paddle set",
      "cheap pickleball paddle"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Beginner Picks
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Pickleball Paddles <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">for Beginners</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Six forgiving paddles and sets under about $120, picked for the things that matter in your first season: a wide sweet spot, a mid weight and a grip that fits.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#selkirk-slk-neo-2-set"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#what-beginners-need" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Beginners Need</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Picks</a>
          <a href="#weight" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Weight</a>
          <a href="#shape" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Shape and Sweet Spot</a>
          <a href="#core-face" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Core and Face</a>
          <a href="#sets" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Sets vs Singles</a>
          <a href="#grip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Grip Size</a>
          <a href="#upgrade" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. When to Upgrade</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A beginner does not need a $250 carbon paddle. You need a forgiving face, a mid weight around 7.6 to 8.2 ounces and a grip that fits your hand, and every paddle below delivers that for well under the price of a premium model.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Chose These Paddles
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not lab-test these paddles. This is a researched buying guide: every weight, core thickness, shape and price below comes from the manufacturer or a named retailer or review listing, and where sources disagree we say so. What we did was decide what a beginner actually needs, then filter the market against that list rather than against the marketing on the box.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first filter was price. A new player does not need a $250 carbon paddle, and most of the performance those paddles add, such as extra spin from raw carbon faces or the power of foam-injected edges, is invisible until your strokes are consistent. We drew the line at roughly $120 for a paddle or a two-player set, with one pick sitting just over that line at full price because it often sells for less.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second filter was forgiveness. A beginner hits the ball off center constantly, so we favored widebody and standard shapes over elongated ones, mid weights over very light or very heavy builds, and cores of 13mm and up. The single elongated paddle on the list is there for players coming from tennis, and we say plainly who should skip it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third filter was legitimacy. Every paddle here is listed as USA Pickleball approved by its maker or its retailers. Approval does not make a paddle good, but it means you can take it to a league night or a local tournament without being told to swap it out, which matters more than most beginners expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are already past the beginner stage, or you want to see how these compare with the paddles intermediate players use, our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">full guide to the best pickleball paddles</a> covers the whole market, including carbon fiber and thermoformed builds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-beginners-need" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What a Beginner Paddle Should Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            A beginner paddle has one job: make the ball go roughly where you meant it to, even when you hit it slightly wrong. That sounds obvious, but it rules out a lot of what gets marketed hardest. Spin ratings, power ratings and pro endorsements describe what a paddle does in skilled hands. For a new player, the sweet spot and the weight matter far more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Control comes first. Most points at the beginner and intermediate level are lost on errors, not won on winners, so a paddle that keeps the ball low and in the court is worth more than one that adds a few miles per hour to a drive. Thicker cores, generally 14mm to 16mm, tend to feel softer and give more control, while thinner 11mm to 13mm cores feel livelier and add pop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Forgiveness is the second need. A wider face gives you more room for error side to side, which is where most beginner mishits land. Widebody paddles sacrifice a little reach, but reach is a problem you will not notice until you can place the ball deliberately. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-choose-a-pickleball-paddle/">how to choose a pickleball paddle</a> walks through how shape, core and face interact if you want the longer version.
          </p>

          <p>
            Comfort is the third. A paddle that is too heavy tires your arm and slows your hands at the kitchen line, and a handle that is too thin or too thick makes you grip harder than you should. Both show up as sore elbows after a few weeks of play, which is the most common reason new players buy a second paddle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
