        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Weight: Why Mid-Weight Is the Safe Start
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paddle weights are usually described in three bands: light paddles under about 7.5 ounces, mid weights from roughly 7.6 to 8.2 ounces, and heavy paddles above that. Most beginner paddles, including most of our picks, sit in or near the middle band, and that is not an accident.
          </p>

          <p>
            Light paddles are quick. They are easy to swing at the kitchen line, where volleys happen fast, and they are kind to sore shoulders. The downside is stability: a light paddle twists more on off-center hits and gives less power on drives, so beginners often compensate by swinging harder, which is how elbow problems start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy paddles are stable and powerful. They plow through the ball and punish fast drives less, but they are slower at the net and harder on the arm over a long session. For a new player who is still learning to keep the paddle up and ready, the extra weight is usually a liability.
          </p>

          <p>
            The middle band gives you most of the stability of a heavier paddle without most of the fatigue. If you are smaller, older or recovering from an arm injury, lean toward the low end; if you are strong and play mostly from the baseline, the top of the band is fine. Our <a href="/pickleball-paddle-weight/">pickleball paddle weight guide</a> covers the trade-offs in more detail, including how to add lead tape later.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical note: listed weights are averages, and individual paddles vary by a few tenths of an ounce. Several of our picks list a range rather than a single figure for exactly that reason. If you buy in person, weigh two or three and pick the one that feels best.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shape" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Shape and Sweet Spot: Widebody vs Elongated
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paddle shape is a trade between width and length. USA Pickleball rules cap the combined length and width, so a paddle that gains reach must give up width, and a paddle that gains width must give up reach. Every shape is a different answer to the same constraint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Widebody paddles, around 8 inches or more across, put the most hitting area where beginners need it. Standard shapes, around 7.8 to 8 inches wide and about 15.5 to 16 inches long, are a sensible middle ground and are what most sets include. Elongated paddles, often 16.5 inches long and 7.5 inches wide or less, add reach and leverage but narrow the sweet spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most beginners, a widebody or standard shape is the right call. The Onix Z5 and HEAD Radical Elite are the widest options on our list, and the Selkirk and JOOLA sets use standard shapes. The Franklin Signature is the exception, and it suits players whose tennis or squash background has already given them consistent contact.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handle length is tied to shape. Widebody paddles often have shorter handles, around 5 inches, which is plenty for one-handed play but cramps a two-handed backhand. Elongated paddles usually have handles of 5.5 inches or more. If you already hit a two-handed backhand, that may decide the shape for you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="core-face" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Core Thickness and Face: What to Ignore for Now
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paddle marketing spends a lot of words on cores and faces. Most of it describes differences a beginner will not feel for months, but two points are worth understanding before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Core thickness affects feel. Thicker cores, typically 16mm, absorb more energy and keep the ball on the face slightly longer, which most players describe as softer and more controllable. Thinner cores, around 13mm, return energy faster and feel poppier. Beginners usually do better on the thicker side, but a 13mm paddle like the SLK Neo is still perfectly easy to learn on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Face material affects power and spin. Fiberglass faces tend to feel springy and give easy power, which helps beginners get the ball deep. Graphite faces feel crisper and slightly more controlled. Raw carbon fiber faces add grit for spin and appear mostly on more expensive paddles, which is why none of our beginner picks use them.
          </p>

          <p>
            What to ignore for now: thermoformed construction, foam-injected edges, spin ratings and branded core names. These matter to intermediate and advanced players chasing specific performance, and they add cost. Our <a href="/how-much-does-a-pickleball-paddle-cost/">guide to what pickleball paddles cost</a> explains what each price tier actually buys you, and where the money starts to make sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Sets vs a Single Paddle
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sets make sense more often than serious players admit. If two people in a household are starting together, a two-paddle set with balls and a bag usually costs less than two separate paddles, and every set on our list uses paddles that are perfectly adequate for the first season or two of play.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is choice. A set gives you two identical paddles chosen by the manufacturer, and set paddles are usually entry-level models. If one of you is coming from tennis and wants an elongated paddle while the other wants a soft widebody, buying singles lets each player choose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check what the balls are. Many sets include outdoor balls, which are harder with smaller holes, and some include indoor balls, which are softer with larger holes. Playing indoor balls outside, or outdoor balls in a gym, changes how the game feels. Our <a href="/best-pickleball-balls/">guide to the best pickleball balls</a> covers which balls suit which courts, and most players end up buying a separate pack anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, read the contents line carefully. Our Niupipo pick is sold in several configurations, and the JOOLA set includes two balls rather than four. A set that looks cheaper may simply include less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Grip Size and Handle Length
          </h2>

          <p>
            Grip size is the circumference of the handle, and most adult paddles fall between about 4 and 4.5 inches. Our picks cluster around 4.25 inches, which suits a wide range of adult hands, with the JOOLA Essentials on the small side at about 4 inches.
          </p>

          <p>
            When in doubt, choose the smaller grip. You can build a small grip up with an overgrip for a few dollars, but you cannot make a thick grip thinner. A grip that is too large forces you to squeeze harder, which tires the forearm, while a grip that is slightly small lets the paddle rotate in your hand on off-center hits.
          </p>

          <p>
            A simple check is to hold the paddle in a handshake grip and see whether you can fit a finger of the other hand between your fingertips and the base of your palm. Our <a href="/pickleball-paddle-grip-size/">pickleball paddle grip size guide</a> has a measuring method and a size chart if you want to be precise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handle length matters mainly for two-handed backhands. Around 5 inches suits one-handed players, while 5.5 inches and up gives room for a second hand. If you are unsure, start one-handed with a standard handle; most beginners do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upgrade" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When to Upgrade, and What to Buy Next
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most players can play happily on a beginner paddle for a season or more. The signs it is time to upgrade are specific: you consistently hit the sweet spot, you want more spin on serves and third shots than the face allows, or the paddle has developed a dead spot that sounds and feels different from the rest of the face.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you upgrade, keep what worked. If a mid-weight widebody felt comfortable, look at mid-weight widebody paddles with carbon faces rather than jumping to an elongated power paddle. The skills you built on a forgiving paddle transfer best to a similar shape.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shoes are the other upgrade most beginners leave too late. Running shoes are built for forward motion and give little support for the side-to-side steps pickleball demands, which is a common route to rolled ankles. Our <a href="/best-pickleball-shoes/">guide to the best pickleball shoes</a> covers court shoes that suit the game, and it is worth reading before you play regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you are ready for a step up, our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddles overall</a> ranks the paddles intermediate players move to, from thermoformed carbon builds to control-focused paddles that reward a developing soft game.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
