          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an Advanced Paddle First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $200-plus carbon paddle with a raw face and a thin, elongated shape is built to reward precise contact. In a beginner's hands it often feels unforgiving and adds little. Spend the difference on good court shoes and balls, and upgrade once you hit the sweet spot consistently and know what you want more of.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing by Power Rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Power and spin ratings describe what a paddle does for a skilled player. For a beginner, most points are lost on errors, so a paddle that adds pace mainly adds unforced errors. Prioritize a wide sweet spot and a mid weight, and let power come from better technique.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Grip Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A grip that is too large makes you squeeze harder and is a common route to a sore forearm or elbow. When unsure, pick the smaller size and build it up with an overgrip, which costs a few dollars and takes minutes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going Too Light</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Very light paddles feel fast in the shop but twist on off-center hits, and beginners often swing harder to compensate. Unless you have an arm or shoulder issue, start in the mid-weight band of roughly 7.6 to 8.2 ounces.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Checking Set Contents</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sets vary more than the photos suggest. Some include two balls, some four; some include indoor balls, some outdoor; some include a bag and some do not. Read the contents line, and check that the balls suit the courts you will actually play on.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

