            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What weight pickleball paddle is best for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most beginners do best with a mid-weight paddle of roughly 7.6 to 8.2 ounces. It gives enough stability to keep off-center hits on target without tiring your arm. Players with shoulder or elbow issues can go slightly lighter, while strong baseline players can go to the top of the range.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a widebody or elongated paddle better for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Widebody or standard shapes are better for most beginners because they offer a wider sweet spot, which forgives the side-to-side mishits new players make most often. Elongated paddles add reach but narrow the sweet spot, so they suit players coming from tennis or squash who already make consistent contact.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much should I spend on my first pickleball paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Somewhere between about $50 and $120 is plenty for a first paddle, and two-player sets in that range cover two people. Premium paddles above $150 add features like raw carbon faces and thermoformed construction that mostly matter once your strokes are consistent.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should beginners buy a pickleball paddle set?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A set is a good buy if two people are starting together, because it usually costs less than two separate paddles and includes balls and a bag. If players want different shapes or weights, buy single paddles instead so each person can choose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do beginner paddles need to be USA Pickleball approved?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For casual play, no. But approval means the paddle meets the rules for sanctioned play, so you can use it in leagues and local tournaments without swapping it. Every paddle in this guide is listed as approved by its maker or retailers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What core thickness is best for a beginner paddle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Thicker cores, around 14 to 16mm, tend to feel softer and more controlled, which helps beginners keep the ball low. Thinner 13mm cores feel livelier and add pop. Either is fine for learning; if you want maximum control, lean toward 16mm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a beginner pickleball paddle last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With regular play, a beginner paddle typically lasts a season or more before the face texture wears or a dead spot develops. Many players upgrade before the paddle wears out, simply because their game has outgrown it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

